# Every layer whose parts are all in its own document, which is the `childLaneSet` population. One
# row per composed layer that carries at least one local part.
#
# The recursion equivalence
#   BPMN already had the element. `Lane` carries an optional `childLaneSet` for sub-partitions,
#   and *a fusion's parts partition what they compose* is that sentence. Rendering a local fusion
#   as a `subProcess` said it was an activity inside a lane; it is a partition of one, and the
#   difference is that a subProcess leaves layer grain while a nested lane does not.
#
# The grain is the whole point
#   A `callActivity` names a process, so a foreign part collapses layer-to-layer into
#   filing-to-filing and the emitted graph can carry a cycle the model does not. A nested lane is
#   still a lane, keyed `(filing, layer)`, so nothing collapses and nothing can be invented. Every
#   part this relation names is a part removed from that exposure.
#
# One row per parent, not per part
#   A `childLaneSet` is one element however many lanes it holds. The child lanes are counted by
#   the `lanes` law, which is unchanged: every layer is still exactly one lane, and nesting moves
#   it rather than duplicating it.
#
# A parent lane holds no `flowNodeRef`s
#   A lane holding a `childLaneSet` must not also hold `flowNodeRef`s, and that is checkable rather
#   than assumed: in this corpus no local fusion carries a draw or a foreign part, so no parent
#   lane owes both. The day one does, the emission owes a decision, and this header is where the
#   reader should be told there was one.
-- diagrams/calls.sqlc pinned to local parts, folded to the parent lane that will hold them.
SELECT DISTINCT c.composition, c.composed_layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.is_local
